<TopicPage slug="subjects/marine-science/foundational/earth-oceans-and-tides">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Marine science starts with the container and the clockwork: how ocean basins came to exist, why
    the sea rises and falls twice a day, and how scientists divide the ocean into zones worth
    studying. The ocean covers 71% of the planet and averages 3.7 km deep — a single, connected
    body of water with geology underneath and astronomy above.
  </p>
  <p>
    Three perspectives organise everything on this page. From <em>above</em>, the ocean is an
    astronomical object: the Moon and Sun raise it, and the rising and falling are among the most
    predictable phenomena in nature. From <em>below</em>, it is a geological feature: water pooled
    in the low places of a crust that is itself being constantly manufactured and destroyed.
    From <em>within</em>, it is a stack of habitats: light, pressure, temperature and food supply
    change faster with depth than with latitude, so marine biologists divide the water column the
    way geographers divide continents. Each perspective has its own units, its own evidence and
    its own surprises — and each is one of the syllabus's three checklist topics.
  </p>

  <h2>Ocean basins and plate tectonics: the container</h2>
  <p>
    The basins exist because Earth's surface recycles itself: new seafloor forms at mid-ocean
    ridges, spreads away, and sinks at trenches. The evidence, assembled in the 1960s, is one of
    geology's cleanest victories. Seafloor <strong>magnetic stripes</strong> — bands of rock
    magnetised in alternating directions, recording Earth's field-reversals as the crust cooled
    and spread — run symmetrically either side of every ridge, a bar-code of creation; core
    samples confirm the stripes are oldest farthest from the ridge; and earthquake foci trace
    the slabs diving back down at the trenches. <strong>Subduction</strong> is the recycling
    step: where oceanic crust (denser, being basalt) meets other crust, it bends down into the
    mantle, dragging a trench with it. That process makes the ocean young relative to the
    planet — no seafloor older than about 200 million years survives, against continents
    nearly 4 billion — and it explains the deepest places, like the Mariana Trench (Challenger
    Deep, about 11 km), as subduction's leftovers.
  </p>
  <p>
    The basin's <em>shape</em> follows the same physics. Around every continent lies a
    <strong>continental margin</strong>: the <em>shelf</em> (a gently sloping underwater
    extension of the continent — narrow off tectonically active coasts like California,
    hundreds of kilometres wide off passive margins like Argentina, and the ocean's richest
    fishing ground because it is shallow enough for light and nutrients), then the
    <em>slope</em> dropping to the <em>rise</em>, built of sediments carried off the shelf by
    gravity flows. Beyond lies the <strong>abyssal plain</strong> — the largest single habitat
    type on Earth, flat because it is blanketed in fine sediment raining down for millions of
    years — interrupted by the <strong>mid-ocean ridge system</strong>, the longest mountain
    chain on the planet (some 65,000 km, mostly submarine), by seamounts (dead volcanoes,
    some coral-capped and biologically rich), and by the trenches. The distribution of land
    itself is a consequence: oceans occupy the low, dense, young basaltic crust; continents
    the high, buoyant, old granitic one — the two crust types are chemically different
    rocks, not different amounts of water.
  </p>

  <h2>Sea level: a moving datum</h2>
  <p>
    Before the tides, a subtler motion: sea level itself is not fixed. <em>Eustatic</em> sea
    level — the global average — moves with the volume of water and the volume of the basins:
    when continents lock up ice ages, sea level drops by well over a hundred metres (at the
    last glacial maximum, about 20,000 years ago, it stood roughly 120–130 m lower, exposing
    land bridges like Beringia and Sunda); when ridge activity is high, the spreading ridges'
    swollen volume displaces water and sea level rises. <em>Local</em> sea level also moves
    for its own reasons — land rebounds after ice sheets melt (isostasy), sinks where
    sediments compact or groundwater is pumped out (Bangkok, Jakarta), and rises with the
    ocean's warming (water expands) and melting ice. This is why "sea level rise" is always a
    two-number story — the ocean rising and the land moving — and why tide gauges must be
    pinned to bedrock to mean anything. For a marine scientist, sea level is the shifting
    boundary condition of every shoreline habitat: the intertidal zone, the subject of later
    units, exists precisely because that boundary moves on every timescale from seconds to
    millennia.
  </p>

  <h2>Tides: the astronomy of the twice-daily breath</h2>
  <Widget no="1" caption="why there are two tides a day" widget="equationStep" config={tidesCfg} />
  <p>
    The two-bulge picture is the first surprise and the first correction to intuition. Gravity
    weakens with distance, so the Moon pulls hardest on the near side of Earth and least on the far
    side; the ocean on the near side is pulled toward the Moon, and the far side is effectively
    left behind — so there are <strong>two bulges, not one</strong>, one facing the Moon and one
    opposite. As the planet spins underneath, most coasts pass through both bulges — two high
    tides and two low tides daily. The Sun raises smaller bulges (about 46% of the Moon's tidal
    force, despite its vastly greater mass, because tide-raising force falls off with the
    <em>cube</em> of distance and the Sun is 400 times farther away). At new and full moon the solar and lunar bulges align
    (<strong>spring tides</strong> — the month's largest ranges, nothing to do with the season);
    at quarter moon they fight (<strong>neap tides</strong> — the smallest). And the Moon advances
    on its orbit as Earth spins, so the lunar day is 24 hours 50 minutes: each day's tides arrive
    about 50 minutes later than the last, the most practical single fact in any tide table.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> tides are not the ocean "sloshing toward the Moon", and the
    bulges are not fixed walls of water. The bulge is a steady deformation of the whole
    water body, and what a coastal observer experiences is the <em>passage</em> of water
    through that deformation as Earth rotates — plus, crucially, the real ocean's response,
    which is a set of <strong>resonant basin oscillations</strong>. Water in a basin rocks
    like water in a bath, with a natural period set by basin depth and length; where that
    natural period matches the tidal forcing, the tide is amplified enormously — the Bay of
    Fundy's 12–16 m range is the classic case, and the geometry of such basins is why some
    coasts (much of the Gulf of Mexico, parts of the Pacific) get <em>one</em> tide a day
    (diurnal) and others almost none, while the equilibrium-bulge theory alone would give
    everyone two. Real tides are the sum of dozens of such <em>constituents</em> — astronomical
    rhythms each written as a sinusoid — added together.
  </p>

  <h2>Tides you can predict with two clocks</h2>
  <p>
    Because tides are astronomical, they are also predictable — the classic tide table is little
    more than the Moon's two rhythms written down. The principal lunar semidiurnal constituent
    runs on a 12.42-hour cycle (the Moon advances on its orbit as Earth spins), and the slower
    diurnal constituent modulates it. Add the two sinusoids and you have a serviceable forecast.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="two tidal constituents added — drag through 48 hours" widget="plotter" config={tideCfg} />

  <p>
    Read the curve like a working forecaster. The fast ripple is the 12.42-hour lunar
    semidiurnal constituent; the slow drift is the 24.07-hour diurnal constituent, which makes
    one of each day's two high tides taller than the other (a <em>mixed tide</em> — the daily
    inequality visible along much of the US Pacific coast). Real predictions add more
    constituents (the Sun's, the lunar orbit's ellipticity — perigean spring tides — and more),
    each with an amplitude and phase measured from years of local gauge data: tide prediction
    is Fourier analysis with a maritime purpose, and the same harmonic method published for
    nineteenth-century ports still drives today's tables and apps. What the method cannot give
    you is the <em>weather's</em> contribution: storm surge (wind and low pressure stacking
    water against the coast — metres of offset in a hurricane) rides on top of the astronomical
    prediction, which is why the most dangerous flooding usually comes when a storm surge
    lands on a spring high tide.
  </p>

  <h2>Classification of the marine environment: slicing the water</h2>
  <p>
    Marine biologists slice the ocean along two axes: distance from shore (neritic versus oceanic)
    and depth (pelagic zones from sunlit epipelagic to abyssal, plus the benthic floor). The
    divisions track physics — light dies within ~200 m, pressure climbs one atmosphere every ten
    metres — so each zone is really a different planet with different rules for life.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>pelagic</strong> (open-water) zones, top to bottom: the
    <strong>epipelagic</strong> (0–200 m), where enough light remains for photosynthesis — the
    ocean's farmland, and home of most of its familiar life; the <strong>mesopelagic</strong>
    (200–1,000 m), the twilight zone where light dwindles to nothing — fish here run vertical
    migrations daily (the largest animal migration on Earth, mostly smaller than your hand);
    the <strong>bathypelagic</strong> (1,000–4,000 m), midnight black, cold (about 2–4 °C) and
    fed entirely by the snow of organic debris from above; the <strong>abyssopelagic</strong>
    (4,000–6,000 m), the abyssal plains' water column; and the <strong>hadopelagic</strong>
    (below 6,000 m), the trenches' own realm, named for Hades. The <strong>benthic</strong>
    label covers the sea floor itself — shelf benthos, abyssal ooze, hydrothermal-vent fields
    — and the shore's intertidal band, the most physically punishing benthic habitat of all.
    Horizontally, the <strong>neritic</strong> province lies over the continental shelf,
    the <strong>oceanic</strong> beyond it; the two differ less in depth than in everything
    depth supplies — neritic waters are sunlit, nutrient-fertilised by runoff and shelf
    mixing, and therefore disproportionately productive.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the deep sea is lifeless, or at least a biological
  desert. It is the opposite in aggregate: with hydrothermal-vent ecosystems running on
  chemistry rather than light (discovered only in 1977), more species than the rainforest
  in sediment samples, and sheer area — the deep benthos covers most of the planet — the
  deep ocean is where most of the biosphere's volume lives. What it lacks is <em>energy</em>,
  not life; its food webs are sparse, slow, and mostly imported.</p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three quantitative details pull the chapter together. First, the <strong>tide-raising
    force scaling</strong>: tidal force goes as the mass of the attracting body divided by the
    <em>cube</em> of its distance — so although the Sun's pull on Earth is ~180 times the
    Moon's, its tide-raising force is only about 0.46 of the Moon's, an arithmetic worth doing
    once because it explains why the Moon, small and near, rules the tides. Second, the
    <strong>resonance arithmetic of Fundy</strong>: the Gulf of Maine–Bay of Fundy system has
    a natural seiche period of roughly 13 hours, close to the 12.42-hour lunar forcing —
    near-resonance amplifies the tide on every cycle, the same physics as pushing a swing at
    its natural rhythm, and the reason coastal engineering (harbours, barrage-tidal power)
    lives or dies on basin geometry. Third, the <strong>basin budget</strong>: the ocean
    covers 71% of Earth's surface but holds ~97% of its water; the remaining ~3% is almost
    all ice (about 1.7%) and groundwater — rivers and lakes are a rounding error. That
    proportion is why ice-sheet behaviour, not rivers, sets eustatic sea level on human
    timescales, and why marine science and climate science have become one subject: the
    container is also the planet's thermostat, its carbon reservoir and, as plate tectonics
    keeps insisting, its most active construction site.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Cambridge International AS &amp; A Level Marine Science 9693 syllabus; NOAA Ocean Exploration; Trujillo &amp; Thurman, <em>Essentials of Oceanography</em>; Pugh &amp; Woodworth, Sea-Level Science (2014).</p>
</TopicPage>
